WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.140
A repeatable prompt engineering workflow begins with a clear task and intended user.

2
00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:10.391
Define the action, input, scope, audience, and success criteria.

3
00:00:10.391 --> 00:00:13.225
Then write a clear primary instruction.

4
00:00:13.225 --> 00:00:18.421
Add context only when it helps the model perform the task.

5
00:00:18.421 --> 00:00:24.089
Add constraints deliberately, and make sure they do not contradict each other.

6
00:00:24.089 --> 00:00:30.229
Specify the output format so the response can be reviewed, reused, or processed.

7
00:00:30.229 --> 00:00:33.063
Use examples when they clarify behavior.

8
00:00:33.063 --> 00:00:36.369
Zero-shot prompts can work for simple tasks.

9
00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:41.565
One-shot and few-shot prompts can show labels, style, transformations, or formatting.

10
00:00:41.565 --> 00:00:46.761
Choose examples carefully because they can introduce bias or accidental constraints.

11
00:00:46.761 --> 00:00:54.318
For complex work, decompose the task into smaller stages with intermediate outputs that can be reviewed.

12
00:00:54.318 --> 00:00:59.986
Treat intermediate data as part of the application workflow, with appropriate protection.

13
00:00:59.986 --> 00:01:02.820
Ground factual tasks with trusted information.

14
00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:12.739
Retrieval and tools can improve relevance and capability, but retrieved content may be untrusted, and tool calls need validation and authorization.

15
00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:15.100
Protect data with least privilege.

16
00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:21.713
Avoid placing sensitive information into prompts unless the system is approved for that use.

17
00:01:21.713 --> 00:01:25.492
High-impact actions should have approval gates and logging.

18
00:01:25.492 --> 00:01:28.326
Finally, test and maintain the prompt.

19
00:01:28.326 --> 00:01:30.687
Use representative and difficult cases.

20
00:01:30.687 --> 00:01:36.355
Measure correctness, completeness, relevance, format compliance, citation quality, safety, cost, and latency.

21
00:01:36.355 --> 00:01:40.134
Combine automated checks with human review where appropriate.

22
00:01:40.134 --> 00:01:45.802
Rerun tests after prompts, models, tools, retrieval sources, or application configuration change.

23
00:01:45.802 --> 00:01:50.997
Prompt engineering is a practical discipline of specification, testing, and control.

24
00:01:50.997 --> 00:02:00.444
A good prompt clarifies intended behavior, but a dependable AI system also needs trusted data, safeguards, evaluation, and ongoing maintenance.

25
00:02:00.444 --> 00:02:03.278
The course workflow is intentionally practical.

26
00:02:03.278 --> 00:02:06.584
Define the task before wording the prompt.

27
00:02:06.584 --> 00:02:13.197
Add context and constraints because they serve a purpose, not because they sound sophisticated.

28
00:02:13.197 --> 00:02:17.448
Use examples when they demonstrate behavior you actually want.

29
00:02:17.448 --> 00:02:20.282
Ground factual work with trusted information.

30
00:02:20.282 --> 00:02:22.643
Protect tools and sensitive data.

31
00:02:22.643 --> 00:02:25.005
Test changes and keep versions.

32
00:02:25.005 --> 00:02:31.618
That is how prompt engineering becomes an engineering practice rather than a guessing game.
